    {
      "id": "tutorial-interface-browser-bar-s4",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Try it",
      "position": {
        "x": -270,
        "y": 460
      },
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 560,
      "data": {
        "title": "Try it",
        "body": "Use the nearby sample note as a quick readout of what the browser bar is telling you. The lesson is intentionally small: read the address, trust the controls, and follow the related GitHub material only when you need the next layer.\n\n## Quick browser-bar checklist\n\nBefore you edit a graph, ask:\n\n- What address am I actually on?\n- Is this `github://`, local, or temporary?\n- Do I trust back, home, and refresh to get me out again?\n- What is the zoom or position indicator telling me, and can it recenter me on the root node?\n- If I need onboarding, is the handshake button on the right side of the address bar where I expect it to be?\n- If I need save actions, is the document-actions menu on the right side of the address bar enough?\n- Do I need the GitHub lesson, or just this interface lesson?\n\nThe browser bar is one of Twilite's main trust surfaces.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "tutorial-interface-browser-bar"
